class:: TGammaRand
summary:: Triggered gamma-distribution sampler
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>Distributions
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/TWeibullRand, Classes/TGaussRand

description::

The Gamma distribution is a continuous probability distribution defined by a shape and scale parameter. Mathematically, it is strictly positive and typically skewed, meaning it frequently produces smaller values but features a heavy tail that occasionally produces very large values. Conceptually, this makes it an excellent choice for generative timing and durations, such as determining the waiting times between unpredictable events, or generating random envelope release times that are usually short and percussive but occasionally ring out for a long time.

Samples the gamma distribution with Marsaglia–Tsang rejection and the standard shape-below-one transform. Rejection is capped at 64 attempts, then the mean code::shape*scale:: is returned.

Like link::Classes/TGaussRand::, it samples on triggers and holds; unlike TGaussRand, it uses a positive gamma law and a local resettable PCG32 sequence.

subsection:: Distribution shape

image::gamma-density.png#Gamma densities with scale = 1 and shape = 0.7, 2, and 5, shown in muted teal, blue, and plum. Shape below one concentrates near zero; larger shape moves the mode outward and makes it more symmetric.::

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: shape
Positive shape. Invalid values use a finite fallback.
argument:: scale
Nonnegative scale. Mean is code::shape*scale::.
argument:: trig
Resample on a rising edge.
argument:: seed
Init-rate local seed.
argument:: reset
Restore the seed and sample a new initial value on a rising edge.
argument:: mul
Output multiplier.
argument:: add
Output offset.

method:: kr
Control-rate version with the same arguments.

instancemethods::

examples::

code::
(
{ TGammaRand.kr(2, 1, Impulse.kr(4)).poll }.play
)
::

code::
(
{ SinOsc.ar(TGammaRand.kr(2, 300, Impulse.kr(8)) + 80, 0, 0.08) }.play
)
::

code::
// Random Skewed Decay Times
(
{
    var trig, decay, sig;
    trig = Impulse.kr(6);
    // Gamma naturally produces mostly short percussive hits, with rare ringing tails
    decay = TGammaRand.kr(shape: 2.0, scale: 0.1, trig: trig);
    sig = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.kr(Env.perc(0.01, decay), trig);
    Pan2.ar(sig * 0.2)
}.play;
)
::
